Subject: Planning brief — Cost-minimal transmission build to link generators and consumers

We are seeking a practical, cost-aware plan to connect two generator sites to nine consumer locations across our service area. In total, there are 11 nodes. We can build transmission lines between any pair of nodes; there are no predefined corridors in this planning exercise. The goal is to determine which lines to build and how to route power so that every consumer’s requirement is met at the lowest combined cost of construction and resistive heating.

Planning data provided:
- Node locations (map coordinates, km): {NODE_COORDINATES}
- Node roles (generator or consumer): {NODE_TYPES}
- Consumer power requirements (MW): {POWER_DEMANDS}
- Generator output limits (MW): {GENERATOR_CAPACITIES}
- Copper wire material specification (resistivity): {WIRE_RESISTIVITY}
- Conductor cross-sectional area: {WIRE_CROSS_SECTION}
- System operating voltage (kV): {OPERATING_VOLTAGE}
- Line thermal rating for power flow in either direction (MW): {LINE_CAPACITY}
- Line build cost per kilometer: {BUILD_COST_PER_KM}
- Monetary valuation of resistive losses: {LOSS_COST_RATE}

Design decisions we need to make:
- Where to build lines: Any two nodes may be connected. Each built line is bidirectional; it can carry power either way up to the specified rating in each direction.
- How much to dispatch from each generator and how to route power across the network to meet all consumer demands.

Key engineering considerations:
- Resistive losses follow a square law. As current increases, I^2R heating rises with the square of current; at a fixed voltage, current is proportional to power flow. This means doubling the power through a line leads to roughly four times the resistive heating on that line. The operational loss on a segment is proportional to its electrical resistance multiplied by the square of power divided by the square of the operating voltage.
- Conductor resistance grows with line length and depends on copper’s material properties and the wire’s cross-section. Longer spans cost more to build and also create more resistance, which increases heating costs when loaded.
- Higher voltage materially reduces current for the same power, thereby reducing I^2R losses with an explicit 1/voltage-squared effect. This is central to keeping ongoing heating costs down.
- Each built line has a rated capacity that caps how much power can pass through it in either direction at any moment. We must respect that limit on every corridor we choose to build.
- Power can only flow across lines that are actually constructed. There is no credit for “virtual” paths.
- Every consumer must be served at or above its stated requirement. Surplus delivery is acceptable. Generators cannot exceed their stated maximum outputs.
- Planning convention: We treat network power flow as conserved at each node (generation plus inflow equals demand plus outflow). Resistive losses are assessed as a separate financial penalty and are not deducted from the nodal balance in this planning model.

Cost structure to be minimized:
- Construction cost: proportional to the distance between connected nodes times the per-kilometer build cost.
- Operational heating cost: based on the square-law relationship above, valuing resistive losses using the provided monetary rate. This is not a linear function of flow; pushing more power through a line quickly becomes expensive because losses grow with the square of the flow.

What success looks like:
- A network plan (which lines to build and how much power to route through each) that meets all consumer needs within generator limits and line ratings, while minimizing the sum of build costs and the cost of resistive losses.
- The plan should leverage the specified operating voltage to limit current and thus reduce heating costs, and it should avoid simplifications that treat losses as linear in flow.
